// Capability witness: does the language enforce NOMINAL TYPE DISTINCTNESS?
//
// Question asked of the existing v2 compiler (gunbc): if two DISTINCT type
// declarations share the SAME structural shape, and a value of one is passed
// where the other is expected, does the compiler REJECT the cross-type call?
//
// UserId and AccountId are two distinct nominal record declarations with an
// identical field shape ({ raw: Int }). Disjointness rides DECLARATION IDENTITY
// (per MODELING / project ledger: a brand desugars to a FRESH nominal type;
// two distinct declarations are disjoint even when structurally identical).
//
// `lookup` expects a UserId. `main` constructs an AccountId and passes it where
// a UserId is required -- the cross-type call.
//
// EXPECTED VERDICT: REJECTED (type error) if nominal distinctness is enforced.
//   The structurally-identical twin in twin.dag FLIPS this to ACCEPTED, proving
//   this witness exercises the real capability (a discriminating input).
//
// Sanity check (frontend parse + typecheck only; the full distinctness verdict
// routes through the not-yet-landed v2-route / candidate-B bridge):
//   gunbc compile --source-root dsl/examples/nominal_distinctness_witness \
//                 --source-root dsl/std --output-dir /tmp/witness_out --target dag
module examples.nominal_distinctness_witness

type UserId {
  raw: Int
}

type AccountId {
  raw: Int
}

fn lookup(id: UserId) -> Int {
  id.raw
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let acct = AccountId { raw: 7 }
  // Cross-type call: AccountId passed where UserId is expected.
  lookup(id: acct)
}
